WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.566 --> 00:00:01.768
♪ (UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYS) 

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:05.405
Thanks for joining us
for the final episode

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.841
of<i> Insecure's</i> after show,
the Wine Down.

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:08.674
Woo!

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:10.977
On this very special occasion,
we are going

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.646
to unpack
our epic series finale,

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:15.681
and why we ended the show
the way we did.

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:17.083
So, Issa, are you ready
to dive in?

00:00:17.249 --> 00:00:18.751
I'm so ready to dive in.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:21.855
-Oh, my God!
-Final clink! Well,
second-to-last clink.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.627 line:0%
So this final episode
goes through

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:30.029 line:0%
a series of time jumps
and tells our story

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:32.465 line:0%
as we pass through months
and even years

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:33.966
of our character's lives.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.103
And now that all is revealed,
we see the way

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.004
that things close out
for our characters.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:42.809
So you wrote the final episode,
which is very fitting,

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:44.777 line:0%
given the history of the show,
of course.

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:46.312 line:0%
So tell us,
in all honesty,

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:48.814 line:0%
when did you know you wanted
Issa to walk

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.116
into the sunset with Lawrence?

00:00:50.282 --> 00:00:52.184
From the very jump
of writing the season.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:55.888
I think I knew that
I didn't want them together,

00:00:56.021 --> 00:00:57.824
and I was very confident
about that.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.025
I was like,
"I want more for our girl."

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:01.327
And I felt like we had
told their love story,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.830
and it ended tragically,
but I still wanted

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:05.164
a healthy love story.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.200
And so I was like,
"It's gonna work

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.234
for Issa and Nathan."

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:09.636
The more and more we started
going into it,

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.003
I was like,
"That's not--"

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.872
I felt like Issa,
like, "I love him

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:15.074
but I know who my guy is."

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.876
We toyed with, like,
"Is it Nathan?

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.077
Is it Lawrence?
Is it Crenshawn?

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.312
Is it--" You know what I mean?
Is it somebody

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:22.381
that we just pick up
that's a random person

00:01:22.481 --> 00:01:24.016
that we haven't followed,
right?

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.185
Because we know we're gonna
end on Issa and Molly, right?

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:27.186
We always knew that.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:30.389
But it still felt like
the level of investment

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:32.825
of the person is gonna be
like the most interesting

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:34.994
version of that, is Lawrence.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:36.529
And you and I talked early on
'cause one of the things

00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:39.031
we wanted to explore
about people being okay, right,

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.867
was this idea
of, "Well, I didn't get

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.535
the things I wanted,"
but is that okay?

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.604
And I think that was
the journey.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.672
I didn't get the season two
fantasy

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.740
I thought I was gonna get
with Lawrence.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.843
The end of season two,
we're moving in, we're married,

00:01:50.943 --> 00:01:52.045
we have a child together.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.781
And in a way, you get that,
it's just not the way

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.081
you thought it was gonna
look like.

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:58.350
But are you gonna be okay?
Yeah, I think so.

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:00.720
Obviously, when you're
ending a series, right,

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.689
there's all this pressure
of finales

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:03.756
that have come before you.

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.558
<i> The Sopranos</i> finale,
and how people feel

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.393
about that,
or<i> Game of Thrones</i> finale.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.729
You don't want to end it
where everything

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.265
is wrapped up all cute,
because that doesn't seem right,

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:13.366
but you also don't want
to end it in a place

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:15.201
where I've been watching
five years, like,

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:16.468
to feel sad.

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:18.037
But you want to be honest,
right?

00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:20.773
And I think Amy Aniobi,
one of our writers

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:23.042
and our director,
really freed us

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.044
from the pressure
of ending the show.

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:27.213
She was like, "Guys,
let's just end it

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.715
as if these character's lives
are just gonna continue on."

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:31.417
It's just weird
jumping out the plane--

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:35.587
We're not landing the plane,
we're just jumping out of it.

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:37.390
And the ride still
continues.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.224
And that's what, I think,
felt really satisfying

00:02:39.324 --> 00:02:40.893
was that it took us--

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:42.394
Sometimes it's not about
where you end up,

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:43.562
it's really how you get there.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:46.332
And say that if you and I,
or us in the room,

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:48.468
are having real debates
in real time,

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:49.568
we want to put that in the show.

00:02:49.735 --> 00:02:51.403
And the debate that
the girls actually have

00:02:51.436 --> 00:02:52.939
in 507 of,
"If I don't want

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.273
to be a dumb bitch,"
you and I actually

00:02:54.406 --> 00:02:56.575
had that dialogue that
you and Tiffany have.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.978
But that is the journey
people have to go through,

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.813
and it took a year for her
or whatever time

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:03.349
for her to process,
"I had to grow."

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:05.418
And we wanted to use
Molly's mom passing,

00:03:05.484 --> 00:03:08.153
not just as the sort of
dramatic thing that happens,

00:03:08.253 --> 00:03:10.256
but it actually kind of,
in some way,

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.592
refocuses our characters
on what's important

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.460
at the end of the day.
But life is too short.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.095
And you sort of say that
when you're talking

00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:17.296
to Lawrence,
when you call,

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:19.198
for his birthday,
and it certainly resonates

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:21.200
at the end of the episode
when you guys are having

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.869
that last moment together
before Molly's wedding.

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:25.838
So... it is really nice
to see all those things

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:28.307
kind of dovetail
for these character's growth.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:33.112
So many tragic life events
have pulled friendships apart,

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:35.781
and I know that we didn't
want that for Issa and Molly,

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.317
but it could still
make it harder to get together

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:42.188
and harder to forge
that bond they've carried

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:43.455
throughout these seasons.

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:45.190
One thing we talk about
in this show

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.627
is this love story
between you and Molly.

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.396
And there's a moment
in the show

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:52.164
when you are helping her
out of her dress,

00:03:52.464 --> 00:03:54.633
but it is the last moment
we see you guys

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.136
actually together
in the frame, right?

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:00.072
So I'm curious what that day
was like,

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.141
and what does it mean
to you to have a show

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:04.243
where for the most part
of this show,

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:06.813
these women were like
the best of friends,

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:07.880
family, like sisters?

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.018
Everything.
I think about my own friendships

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:15.387
and I think about my friendship
that's developed with Yvonne

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:18.657
over the course of six years
creating this.

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:20.259
And it was such an
emotional day.

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:23.161
It was Issa sending
her friend off

00:04:23.328 --> 00:04:24.830
into the next chapter
of her life.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:28.433
It was this moment of them
kind of reckoning

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.004
and acknowledging everything
they had been through.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:36.175
They found each other,
and they've loved each other

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:38.578
through all of their flaws,
and that "Thank you"

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:39.611
meant so much.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:44.516
When we started season one,
could you imagine

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.452
that fans would be
so invested,

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:49.422
or that they'd harass you
online or in-person,

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:51.624
to make the show longer,
or get mad

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.893
when Issa made life decisions,
or dated certain men?

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:57.797
Did you think it would
just be this?

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:00.265
I mean, you know,
we were in such a vacuum

00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:02.434
in that first season,
especially.

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:05.437
We just wanted
to stay on the air.

00:05:05.537 --> 00:05:07.073
We just wanted to tell
our stories

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.709
and tell it the way
we wanted to.

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:11.744
You know, you imagine
this audience

00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:13.379
of family and friends
and things like that,

00:05:13.412 --> 00:05:15.047
but you don't know
who's going to be watching

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:16.549
the show,
and you hope they like it.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:20.219
But I get fake annoyed,
I get real annoyed,

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.054
but it really is
a true blessing

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:23.889
to have people
so invested.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.991
These characters are a part
of people's lives

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:27.259
in such a rich way.

00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:29.062
And you can only dream
of creating

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:30.262
characters like that.

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:31.964
You know, a lot of times
if you're

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:34.600
in the white world of Hollywood,
if you're making a Black show

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:36.268
it's like, "Let's get
every Black person

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.570
every Black person knows
already."

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:38.637
'Cause we just want
to make sure

00:05:38.737 --> 00:05:39.739
people know these people,
right?

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:41.240
But white shows kind
of get a chance

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:43.842
to take a chance on an indie
person you never heard of

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:44.743
before, right?

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:45.778
And I think that was
a thing

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:47.246
that I thought you were
so smart in the beginning,

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:48.947
was, "Let's do what
Spike Lee did

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:50.916
and find people that will
grow out of that,

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.920
and the next wave
of Black talent."

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:56.588
And so, you got
to personalize

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:58.924
and make Issa be whoever
you wanted Issa to be,

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:00.660
for yourself, same with Molly,
same with Lawrence.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.594
It's like they felt like
every-people, right?

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.431
And so I think that helped
always keep the show

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:08.800
feeling like, "Oh,
that's not Denzel Washington

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.970
as his dad."
So I think that helps you

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:12.504
become more invested.

00:06:12.771 --> 00:06:14.640
When we first met
and sat down,

00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:16.208
not too far from here,
actually,

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:19.645
for me, my goal was always,
"Hey, I want to make sure

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:21.546
you get to tell the stories
you want to tell."

00:06:21.713 --> 00:06:23.483
But you obviously had
a different challenge, too,

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:26.618
because you had to adapt it
from a web-series, right?

00:06:26.985 --> 00:06:29.121
Into a totally different form
in so many ways.

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:32.525
The web-series was what it was,
and I wanted to create

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:35.228
something new for this network.

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.630
And I don't know that I had
the expectations

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.833
of where I wanted
the series to go,

00:06:40.966 --> 00:06:42.634
where I wanted every
character to go.

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:45.204
I knew the journey I wanted
to take them on,

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:50.543
but this has exceeded
everything I imagined

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.177
that it would be
in the best way.

00:06:52.177 --> 00:06:54.913
And I'm just forever grateful
for that.

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:56.682
What I think is so great
about this show

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:59.585
is getting to watch it
change and grow

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:00.920
with each person
that we brought on.

00:07:01.153 --> 00:07:03.989
So I read the script one way,
but then we brought Melina on,

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:06.926
and it elevated it
to another place.

00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:09.328
And we just continue to see
this thing grow and grow.

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:10.696
So just to piggy-back
your point,

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:11.764
I totally agree with that.

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:14.333
Yeah, the people
behind the scenes

00:07:14.366 --> 00:07:16.335
-made the show what it is.
-PRENTICE PENNY: Agree.

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.904
You know, the city in which
we both grew up, L.A.,

00:07:19.004 --> 00:07:20.506
is a huge part of the show.

00:07:21.173 --> 00:07:22.541
And we always talked about
the way

00:07:22.674 --> 00:07:25.277
that Scorsese or Spike Lee
or Woody Allen

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:27.880
sort of make these
love letters to New York, right?

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:30.049
Why was it important for you
to make a show

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:32.518
that was a sort of love letter,
not just to L.A.,

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:33.886
but the specific part
of L.A.

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.354
that normally doesn't
get seen?

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.556
I think it's that.
It's what you just said,

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:40.359
and I feel like L.A.
is always represented

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:42.861
as either Hollywood,
the glamour and glitz,

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.364
or the hood,
and the in-between

00:07:45.531 --> 00:07:48.134
doesn't really exist,
especially in between

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:49.568
as it relates to us.

00:07:50.235 --> 00:07:54.306
And that was so important
for me to portray.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:56.641
The finale was initially,
half of it

00:07:56.775 --> 00:07:58.311
was going to be in Morocco,
three-fourths of it

00:07:58.411 --> 00:08:00.813
was gonna be in Morocco,
just to pay off

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:03.715
Issa and Molly's
the fantasy destination.

00:08:03.815 --> 00:08:06.384
And it was a great episode,
but it wasn't in L.A.

00:08:06.485 --> 00:08:07.886
and it just didn't
feel right.

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:10.389
So we wanted to give L.A.
a good ending as well.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:12.891
Yeah, L.A., we also had
a Rihanna impersonating--

00:08:13.058 --> 00:08:15.661
(CHUCKLES) Ribabba!
R.I.P. Ribabba!

00:08:15.794 --> 00:08:19.765
R.I.P. Ribabba! Our fake
Rihanna concert in Morocco.

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:22.435
-So...
-So, Prentice, in your opinion,

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:25.638
is Issa finally secure?

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.841
I think so.
But to say the question,

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:31.510
the word, "final"
has a weird thing on it, right?

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:33.145
I think you and I
have always talked about,

00:08:33.245 --> 00:08:35.181
and I think we talked
about this day one,

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:38.417
about the series,
was that the real ending

00:08:38.483 --> 00:08:40.619
of the show was when
you get secure

00:08:40.753 --> 00:08:42.154
in your insecurities,
and knowing that

00:08:42.288 --> 00:08:43.855
insecurities won't ever
leave, right?

00:08:44.022 --> 00:08:47.426
And so it's understanding that,
"I'll go through ups and downs

00:08:47.459 --> 00:08:48.460
and all that,
but I don't have

00:08:48.593 --> 00:08:49.461
to have these sweeping
reactions."

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:51.329
In the first two seasons,
Issa's always like,

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.666
"This thing happens,
I'mma be a no-fucks bitch.

00:08:53.766 --> 00:08:55.101
Or I'mma be a this,
I'mma be a that."

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:56.335
As opposed to being like,
"You know what?

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:58.437
I'm gonna ride this wave
a little bit,

00:08:58.504 --> 00:09:00.873
and just be uncomfortable
as it's uncomfortable

00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:01.873
and then grow out of it."

00:09:02.274 --> 00:09:04.776
And so, if I was to say,
I think she finally recognizes

00:09:04.876 --> 00:09:07.112
that real security comes
from understanding

00:09:07.145 --> 00:09:10.850
there kind of is no final
version of being fully secure.

00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:12.184
Well said.

00:09:12.851 --> 00:09:15.687
To our dedicated viewers,
we thank you guys

00:09:15.787 --> 00:09:18.291
for riding with us
for the past five seasons.

00:09:18.457 --> 00:09:20.726
And we hope that you enjoyed
this journey

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:22.962
as much as we did.
We will miss you.

00:09:23.129 --> 00:09:26.798
<i> Insecure,</i> we hope,
lives in your hearts forever.

00:09:26.998 --> 00:09:28.700
Thank you, guys,
for rocking with us,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.202
and for letting
these characters

00:09:30.335 --> 00:09:31.804
into your homes
every Sunday.

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:33.705
Thank you.
Nobody has to tweet,

00:09:33.805 --> 00:09:34.973
"Make it an hour" anymore.

00:09:35.073 --> 00:09:36.542
(LAUGHING)

00:09:36.976 --> 00:09:38.877
-Thank y'all.
-Bye, y'all, thank you.

00:09:38.977 --> 00:09:40.346
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

